get [SimTalk]
Returns the value of the Python variable of the PythonModule designated by <Path>.
Remarks
The method get only converts those Python data types, which can be converted to the SimTalk data types
string, real, integer, boolean, time, date, and dateTme. The method returns all other Python data types as
the SimTalk data type any, allowing you to address the specified PythonModule.
You can access the PythonModule object as follows:
<Path>.get(VariableName:string) -> any
// Returns the value of the variable with the name VariableName.
<Path>.set(VariableName:string, Value:<any>) -> any
// Sets the value of the variable with the name VariableName to Value.
<Path>.callMethod(MethodName:string[, Parameter:any, ...]) -> any
// Calls the method MethodName of the Python object with the specified 
paramters.
<Path>.call([Parameter:any, ...]) -> any
// Calls the Python object with the specified paramters.
Type
Method
Syntax
<Path>.get(VariablenName:string) -> any
Parameter
The parameter VariableName of data type string designates the name of the Python variable.
Return Value
The return value has the data type any.
get [SimTalk]
11-3754 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Example
Suppose our Python code in the PythonModule looks like this:
class ValuePair:
def __init__(self, Val1, Val2):
      self.Val1 = Val1
      self.Val2 = Val2
def getSumString(self, prefix, suffix):
      return prefix + str(self.Val1 + self.Val2) + suffix
        
vp = ValuePair(12, 23)
def calcArea(x, y):
    return x*y
valList = [1,2,3]
valDict = {None: ['Row1', 'Row2'], 'Col1': [None, 12], 'Col2': ['C21', 
'C22']}
Our SimTalk code, which we call in a Method, looks like this:
var po = PythonModule.get("vp")
print po.get("Val1") // -> 12
print po.get("Val2") // -> 23
print po.callMethod("getSumString", "Sum=", "m") // returns "Sum=35m"
var calcArea = PythonModule.get("calcArea")
print calcArea.call(3, 4)
var valList = PythonModule.get("valList")
print valList.call(__len__) // -> 3 Returns the number of elements in the 
Python list object
print valList[1]            // -> 2 Returns the second element of the 
Python list object (Python lists start with index 0)
var valDict = PythonModule.get("valDict")
print valDict["Col1"]       // returns [None, 12]
valDict["Col1"] = [11,12]   // Assign a new value to the Python dictionary 
object
print valDict["Col1"]       // returns [11, 12]
